# 📊 FinQA LLM Evaluation

This project extends the FinQA benchmark by evaluating modern AI systems such as **ChatGPT, Gemini, and GitHub Models (Copilot-style inference)** on financial numerical reasoning tasks.

Instead of using the original program-based evaluation from FinQA, this project introduces a simplified and practical evaluation pipeline that compares model outputs directly against ground-truth answers while also measuring response time.

---

## 🚀 Project Overview

The original FinQA paper evaluates models based on **program generation and execution**. However, modern large language models (LLMs) do not naturally produce structured programs.

This project proposes a new approach:

- ✅ Evaluate **final answers instead of programs**
- ✅ Compare **multiple AI models**
- ✅ Measure both **accuracy and response time**
- ✅ Analyze **financial reasoning performance**

---

## ⚙️ Setup Instructions

### 1. Install dependencies

```bash
python -m pip install -r requirements_models.txt
2. Set API Keys (PowerShell)

Before running the scripts, set your API keys:

$env:OPENAI_API_KEY="XXXXXXXXXXXX"
$env:GEMINI_API_KEY="AAAAAAAAAAAAA"
$env:GITHUB_TOKEN="CCCCCCCCCCCCC"

⚠️ These keys are temporary and reset when the terminal is closed.

▶️ How to Run
🔹 Run live comparison (single question)
python ask_live_compare.py --providers openai gemini github_models
🔹 Run dataset experiment
python multi_model_compare.py --dataset dev.json --providers openai gemini github_models --limit 5 --out_dir outputs
🔹 Evaluate all models
python eval_all_models.py --dataset dev.json --predictions_dir outputs --out_dir eval_outputs
📊 Generating Results for Analysis (IMPORTANT)

This section explains how to generate the results used in the Results and Analysis section of the paper.

Step 1 — Run the experiment
python multi_model_compare.py --dataset dev.json --providers openai gemini github_models --limit 10 --out_dir outputs

This will:

Run multiple FinQA questions
Query all selected models
Save predictions to the outputs/ folder
Step 2 — Run evaluation
python eval_all_models.py --dataset dev.json --predictions_dir outputs --out_dir eval_outputs

This will:

Compare predictions with ground truth
Compute accuracy
Measure response time
Step 3 — View results

Go to:

eval_outputs/

Open:

all_models_summary.csv
📈 Example Output
Model                   Total  Correct   Accuracy     Avg Time  Median Time  Missing IDs   Parse Fail
